{
  "id": "ep093",
  "duration": 47.041,
  "beats": [
    {
      "start": 0.08,
      "end": 3.084,
      "text": "Think the Moon doesn't spin? That is a total myth.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Bill Anders",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 0.0,
          "end": 3.1,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 3.084,
      "end": 10.104,
      "text": "Our Moon actually rotates on its axis at the exact same speed it orbits Earth, a perfect celestial match.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Solar eclipse taken from Artemis II - April 2026.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Solar_eclipse_taken_from_Artemis_II_-_April_2026.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 3.1,
          "end": 5.6,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 5.6,
          "end": 10.1,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 10.104,
      "end": 17.449,
      "text": "This synchronous tidal locking is caused by Earth's gravity stretching the lunar body into subtle, physical bulges.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Tidal bulge.png",
        "credit": "Orion 8",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tidal_bulge.png"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 10.1,
          "end": 17.433,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 17.449,
      "end": 25.419,
      "text": "As the Moon spins, these bulges get carried slightly out of alignment, generating a powerful gravitational braking torque.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wellcome Collection",
        "title": "Astronomy: eclipses (top), and the Moon's passage around the Earth. Coloured engraving by J. Emslie, 1851, after himself",
        "credit": "Wellcome Collection",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://wellcomecollection.org/works/dmzeqfcb"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 17.433,
          "end": 20.867,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 20.867,
          "end": 25.433,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 25.419,
      "end": 32.539,
      "text": "This constant friction slows its rotation and pushes the Moon away from us by four centimeters every year.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Released to Public Earth by NASA (NASA, GPN-2000-001437) (241635550).jpg",
        "credit": "pingnews.com",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Released_to_Public_Earth_by_NASA_(NASA,_GPN-2000-001437)_(241635550).jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 25.433,
          "end": 32.533,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 32.539,
      "end": 41.014,
      "text": "Because of this locking, humanity did not see the far side until the Soviet probe Luna 3 photographed it in 1959.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "Luna-3 (Memorial Museum of Astronautics).JPG",
        "credit": "Armael",
        "license": "CC0",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Luna-3_(Memorial_Museum_of_Astronautics).JPG"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 32.533,
          "end": 35.667,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        },
        {
          "start": 35.667,
          "end": 41.0,
          "kind": "detail",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "start": 41.014,
      "end": 46.691,
      "text": "Tidal locking hides the far side, which is why people still believe the Moon never rotates.",
      "asset": {
        "source": "Wikimedia Commons",
        "title": "NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg",
        "credit": "NASA/Bill Anders",
        "license": "Public domain",
        "url": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:NASA-Apollo8-Dec24-Earthrise.jpg"
      },
      "shots": [
        {
          "start": 41.0,
          "end": 47.033,
          "kind": "card",
          "motion": "camera"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
